---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import cases from '../data/cross_domain.json';
const W=900,L=206,R=68,ROW=28,H=cases.avoidds.length*ROW+55;
const mx=x=>L+(x-.55)/(.45)*(W-L-R);
const nasaW=900,nasaL=205,nasaR=65;
const nx=x=>nasaL+x/3.5*(nasaW-nasaL-nasaR);
---
<SiteLayout title="Cross-domain cases">
  <h1>Same budget question. <span style="color:var(--cyan)">Different failure modes.</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">Road autonomy, aircraft detect-and-avoid, and face verification expose different constraints: sensor information, training coverage, output meaning, operating domain, and compute. The observed numbers below keep their original units and experimental scope.</p>

  <section class="card">
    <div class="kicker">Detect-and-avoid / training coverage</div><h2>Same 11.2M-parameter model family, different data</h2>
    <p>AVOIDDS trained two YOLOv8s detectors of the same architecture: one on the 64,800-image training split of a 72,000-image synthetic dataset, one on a 6,944-image nominal subset. The figure plots their test-set mAP in 23 reported slices. This tests data coverage and operating conditions while holding architecture size fixed.</p>
    <svg class="chart" viewBox={`0 0 ${W} ${H}`} role="img" aria-label="Twenty-three AVOIDDS slices comparing baseline and restricted-training YOLOv8s mean average precision">
      {[.6,.7,.8,.9,1].map(t=><g><line class="gridline" x1={mx(t)} x2={mx(t)} y1={20} y2={H-32}/><text x={mx(t)} y={H-9} text-anchor="middle">{Math.round(t*100)}%</text></g>)}
      {cases.avoidds.map((r,i)=>{const y=30+i*ROW;return <g><text x={L-10} y={y+4} text-anchor="end">{r.group==='Overall'?r.slice:`${r.group}: ${r.slice}`}</text><line x1={mx(r.alternative_map)} x2={mx(r.baseline_map)} y1={y} y2={y} stroke="#607b84" stroke-width="2"/><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.11203" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><circle cx={mx(r.alternative_map)} cy={y} r="5" fill="#ff8295"><title>{`${r.slice}: restricted-data model ${(r.alternative_map*100).toFixed(1)}% mAP · ${r.alternative_id} · ${r.source_locator}`}</title></circle><circle cx={mx(r.baseline_map)} cy={y} r="5" fill="#5de0d4"><title>{`${r.slice}: full-data model ${(r.baseline_map*100).toFixed(1)}% mAP · ${r.baseline_id} · ${r.source_locator}`}</title></circle></a></g>})}
    </svg>
    <p><span style="color:var(--cyan)">●</span> full-data baseline &nbsp; <span style="color:var(--rose)">●</span> restricted-data alternative. Each of 23 paired slices supplies two plotted observations. The slices overlap and are not independent samples.</p>
    <p>The restricted-data model has higher overall test-set recall (92.8% versus 90.7%) but lower mAP and a higher simulated NMAC frequency. A single aggregate detection metric cannot stand in for the downstream outcome.</p>
    <div class="grid"><div class="bigreadout"><strong>Overall detection mAP</strong><br/>86.6% versus 76.4% on the AVOIDDS test set.</div><div class="bigreadout"><strong>Simulated near mid-air collision frequency</strong><br/>14.3% versus 16.5% across 8,640 simulated encounters.</div></div>
    <p class="source">Original: <a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.11203" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Smyers et al., AVOIDDS, §3.2, Table 4 and Table 5</a>. These are synthetic-data and closed-loop simulation results. They do not establish real-world collision risk, standard compliance, or a causal effect solely from image count.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Detect-and-avoid / sensor geometry</div><h2>Detection range is conditional on the target</h2><p>NASA tested one 4K, 41° field-of-view camera in head-on flight encounters. The six reported first-detection ranges below use the same sensor configuration but two different target classes. The horizontal bars show the paper's fit error, not a universal uncertainty interval.</p>
    <svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 900 295" role="img" aria-label="NASA flight-test first detection range across six sorties, small UAS and SR22 targets">
      {[0,1,2,3].map(t=><g><line class="gridline" x1={nx(t)} x2={nx(t)} y1="20" y2="251"/><text x={nx(t)} y="276" text-anchor="middle">{t} km</text></g>)}
      {cases.nasa_sorties.map((r,i)=>{const y=37+i*36;return <g><text x={nasaL-10} y={y+4} text-anchor="end">{r.target==='small UAS'?'small UAS':'SR22'} · sortie {r.sortie}</text><line x1={nx(r.range_km-r.fit_error_km)} x2={nx(r.range_km+r.fit_error_km)} y1={y} y2={y} stroke="#9ab7ff" stroke-width="3"/><a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20205011011" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><circle cx={nx(r.range_km)} cy={y} r="7" fill={r.target==='small UAS'?'#ff8295':'#5de0d4'}><title>{`${r.range_km} ± ${r.fit_error_km} km; ${r.target}, sortie ${r.sortie}; ${r.id}; ${r.source_locator}`}</title></circle></a></g>})}
    </svg>
    <p class="source">Original: <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20205011011" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Dolph et al., Table 3 and §VI</a>. Weighted means: 0.881 ± 0.061 km for the small UAS and 2.763 ± 0.037 km for the SR22; these means are derived from the six plotted sorties, not extra independent tests.</p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Detect-and-avoid / learned cascade</div><h2>AirTrack</h2><p>Its pipeline aligns frames, runs cascaded detection, applies a secondary ResNet-18 classifier, then a 2D Kalman filter. The paper reports 2448×2048 grayscale frames, with a photographed aircraft narrower than 15 pixels in an example image.</p><div class="stat">&gt;95% to 700 m</div><p>Reported track probability over range in the AOT evaluation. On a Jetson AGX Xavier development kit, the reported full vision setup ran at 66.8 FPS with TensorRT and 5.3 FPS without it. These are workload and implementation measurements, not model FLOPs or a flight-approval finding.</p><p class="source"><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2209.12849" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original AirTrack paper: Fig. 2, Fig. 5, §V</a></p></section>
    <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Detect-and-avoid / benchmark construction</div><h2>AVOIDDS</h2><p>72,000 photorealistic synthetic images vary lighting, weather, aircraft type, geometry and geography. A test set measures detection; a closed-loop simulator measures downstream encounter outcomes. The results above show why both are needed.</p><div class="stat">72,000 images</div><p>A large image count alone does not show how many independent scenarios or rare encounters are covered. The amount and location of variation matter.</p><p class="source"><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.11203" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original AVOIDDS paper: abstract, Tables 1–5</a></p></section>
  </div>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">A fourth architecture / no comparable hardware datum</div><h2>Detector, tracker and learned depth estimate</h2><p><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/2405.06749" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karampinis et al. (2024)</a> describe a vision-only aircraft pipeline with detection, appearance/Kalman tracking and a separate lightweight encoder-decoder depth-estimation model, evaluated on the AOT dataset. It is a useful architectural contrast with AirTrack's range/track pipeline. We have not added it to the capacity graph because the inspected paper does not give a matched, comparable whole-pipeline parameter and target-device throughput measurement.</p></section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Automotive / published and projected hardware</div><h2>Tesla HW3, AI4 and proposed AI5</h2><p>These rows show what public primary sources actually constrain. HW3 is a 2019 vendor architecture disclosure. Tesla's AI4 absolute compute and memory capacities are not supplied by the cited 2026 investor update. AI5 is a forward-looking relative target; absolute TOPS cannot be inferred without an AI4 baseline and compatible arithmetic definitions.</p>
    <div class="tablewrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Public quantity</th><th>Status</th><th>What the number can support</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><td>HW3 / FSD computer</td><td>72 trillion operations/s per chip; 144 trillion operations/s across two chips</td><td>Tesla 2019 vendor presentation</td><td>Two independent chips each receive video; aggregate 144 is not one larger model's capacity. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Ucp0TTmvqOE&t=4969s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI4 / HW4</td><td>Absolute peak compute and effective throughput: undisclosed in the cited investor update</td><td>Production generation</td><td>A required missing calibration point, not a zero on the graph. <a href="https://ir.tesla.com/_flysystem/s3/sec/000162828026003837/tsla-20260128-gen.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td>AI5 / often called HW5</td><td>Targets relative to AI4: 10× raw compute, 9× memory capacity, 5× hardened block quantization/softmax; 50× total performance claim</td><td>Forward-looking Tesla target, January 2026</td><td>Relative roadmap only, not measured throughput or shipped capability. Tesla then planned production in 2027. <a href="https://ir.tesla.com/_flysystem/s3/sec/000162828026003837/tsla-20260128-gen.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <p>Tesla's <a href="https://ir.tesla.com/_flysystem/s3/sec/000162828026026551/tsla-20260422-gen.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">April 2026 update, page 8</a> says AI5 final chip design was completed and reports up to 20% lower FSD runtime inference latency after a compiler/runtime rewrite. Its <a href="https://ir.tesla.com/_flysystem/s3/sec/000162828026049213/tsla-20260722-gen.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">July 2026 update, page 8</a> says FSD v14 lite distills AI4 v14 driving behavior into AI3 camera and compute constraints. Both are vendor software/development statements; they do not reveal the AI4 capacity ceiling or measured AI5 throughput.</p>
    <h3>AI5 target index, AI4 = 1</h3><p>Different quantities appear on separate rows; the 50× total-performance claim is not obtained by multiplying the displayed ratios.</p>
    <svg class="chart" viewBox="0 0 900 240" role="img" aria-label="Tesla AI5 targets relative to AI4: ten times raw compute, nine times memory capacity, five times hardened operations, fifty times total performance claim">
      {[["Raw compute",10],["Memory capacity",9],["Hardened operations",5],["Total performance claim",50]].map(([name,val],i)=>{const y=35+i*48;return <g><text x="180" y={y+6} text-anchor="end">{name}</text><rect x="198" y={y-11} width={Number(val)/50*610} height="22" rx="4" fill={i===3?'#ffc878':'#5de0d4'} opacity={i===3?'.8':'.7'}/><text x={210+Number(val)/50*610} y={y+6}>{val}×</text></g>})}
    </svg>
    <p class="source">Source: <a href="https://ir.tesla.com/_flysystem/s3/sec/000162828026003837/tsla-20260128-gen.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Tesla Q4 2025 Update, page 10</a>. Tesla calls FSD supervised; <a href="https://www.tesla.com/support/ai-computer" target="_blank" rel="noopener">its support page</a> states vehicles require active driver supervision.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Other edge compute anchors</div><h2>Published peaks need precision labels</h2><div class="tablewrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Processor</th><th>Published peak</th><th>Why it is not a fit result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jetson AGX Xavier</td><td>32 INT8 TOPS</td><td>NVIDIA technical brief reference value; pipeline utilization must be measured. <a href="https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/gtcf21/jetson-orin/nvidia-jetson-agx-orin-technical-brief.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>Jetson AGX Orin 64GB</td><td>up to 275 <strong>sparse INT8</strong> TOPS; 204 GB/s bandwidth</td><td>Sparse peak cannot be compared directly with dense FP GFLOPs. <a href="https://www.nvidia.com/content/dam/en-zz/Solutions/gtcf21/jetson-orin/nvidia-jetson-agx-orin-technical-brief.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>DRIVE AGX Orin, one SoC</td><td>up to 254 INT8 TOPS; up to 200 GB/s</td><td>Vendor peaks, not an end-to-end driving-system measurement. <a href="https://developer.nvidia.com/drive/agx" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section>

  <section class="card"><div class="kicker">Face verification / benchmark saturation</div><h2>Small models can succeed on a narrow task</h2><div class="tablewrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th>Recognizable case</th><th>Reported LFW accuracy</th><th>Additional bound or condition</th><th>Original source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>DeepFace (2014)</td><td>97.35%</td><td>Face verification system; training data and protocol differ from later work</td><td><a href="https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content_cvpr_2014/html/Taigman_DeepFace_Closing_the_2014_CVPR_paper.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVPR paper ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>FaceNet (2015)</td><td>99.63%</td><td>Embedding and triplet-loss system</td><td><a href="https://www.cv-foundation.org/openaccess/content_cvpr_2015/papers/Schroff_FaceNet_A_Unified_2015_CVPR_paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVPR paper ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>MobileFaceNet (2018)</td><td>99.55%</td><td>0.99M parameters, 221M multiply-adds, 4.0 MB stored model; 92.59% MegaFace TAR at FAR 10⁻⁶ under the paper's large protocol</td><td><a href="https://arxiv.org/pdf/1804.07573" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Paper abstract, §4, Tables 3–4 ↗</a></td></tr><tr><td>ArcFace (2019)</td><td>99.83%</td><td>Reported LFW result for a particular backbone/training setup</td><td><a href="https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content_CVPR_2019/papers/Deng_ArcFace_Additive_Angular_Margin_Loss_for_Deep_Face_Recognition_CVPR_2019_paper.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVPR paper ↗</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="source">LFW is close to ceiling for these systems. Its accuracy should not be treated as an operating point for a deployed identification threshold. MegaFace TAR at a specified FAR is closer to a thresholded ROC reading, but benchmark conditions and demographic/quality slices still matter.</p></section>
</SiteLayout>
